Critically, activating any depot-to-unit route on a given day (i.e., sending any vehicles along that route) incurs a fixed Risk Cost representing the security overhead described above, regardless of how many vehicles are dispatched. No vehicles may use a route unless it is activated. In addition, transportation costs apply to the commodities moved, and each vehicle deployed carries its own operating cost. Fleet availability is finite.

Mission:
Deliver required quantities of fuel, ammunition, and medical supplies from rear depots to designated forward elements over the planning horizon at minimum total operational cost, while maintaining operational tempo and within fleet and depot constraints.

Execution/Concept:
- Depot outflows are limited by daily availability at each site by commodity. Prioritize sourcing from depots with adequate stocks and favorable transport costs to reduce haul distances and risk exposure.
- For each forward element and commodity by day, meet demand where possible. Shortfalls are permitted only when constrained by stocks, risk, route availability, or fleet limits, and are assessed a shortage penalty representing operational impact.
- Movement planning is vehicle-centric. Each commodity has a defined volume that translates to vehicle loading; each vehicle has a fixed capacity and a per-day deployment cost. Total vehicles employed per day cannot exceed the fleet limit.
- Vehicles can only use active routes. Activating a route on any day triggers a fixed Risk Cost independent of vehicle count, capturing the need for escorts, route clearance, ISR, and QRF coverage. Once active, multiple vehicles may use the route subject to capacity and fleet constraints.
- Transportation costs accrue by commodity and route. The plan should concentrate flows on a minimal set of secure routes to amortize fixed risk costs, push high-penalty commodities first, and avoid activating low-yield routes unless required to prevent critical shortfalls.
- The objective is to minimize the sum of transportation costs, shortage penalties, per-vehicle deployment costs, and fixed route security Risk Costs, while adhering to depot supply limits, vehicle capacity, route activation rules, and fleet constraints.

